In C, there are several ways to terminate a program. Here are a few common methods:

### 1. Using the `exit()` function

The `exit()` function is a standard C function that terminates the program immediately. It takes an integer argument that specifies the exit status of the program.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    // Your code here
    exit(0); // Exit with a successful status
    // exit(1); // Exit with an error status
    return 0;
}
```

### 2. Using the `_Exit()` function

The `_Exit()` function is similar to `exit()`, but it does not return any value to the operating system. It is generally used when you want to terminate the program without returning a value.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    // Your code here
    _Exit(0); // Exit with a successful status
    // _Exit(1); // Exit with an error status
    return 0;
}
```

### 3. Using the `abort()` function

The `abort()` function terminates the program immediately and reports an error to the operating system. It is typically used when an error occurs that cannot be recovered

To terminate a C program, you have a few options.  The most common one is to use the `exit()` function.  Here’s an example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    printf(“Hello, world!\n”);
    exit(0);  // This will terminate the program
    return 0;
}
```

Another way to terminate a program is to use the `abort()` function, which will also terminate the program and print an error message:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    printf(“Hello, world!\n”);
    abort();  // This will terminate the program and print an error message
    return 0;
}
```

